$ jailops app export cache
# cache: an app definition for the JailOps catalog, format version 1 (docs/apps.md).
# Fill in what is marked; 'jailops app validate cache.yaml' lists each marked line left.
# To try it on a host, copy cache.yaml and its icon into /usr/local/etc/jailops/apps and run
# 'jailops app install cache'.
# Drafted from the jail cache with 'jailops app export'. What the jail showed:
# - packages: memcached (installed on purpose; their dependencies come with them)
# - services: memcached (enabled in /etc/rc.conf)
# - ports: 11211/tcp (listening now)
# - ports of the base system left out: 22/tcp of sshd
# - data: /var/db/memcached (the jail's volumes)
# - setup: the owners were read from the jail (pkg -r leaves the directories of a package's user to root)

# Format version of the definition; 1.
version: 1

# The app name: a jail name (^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,62}$), equal to the file name without .yaml.
name: cache

# One line, at most 80 characters.
title: cache

# One line, at most 140 characters: what it is and what it is for.
summary: "TODO: what it is and what it is for, in one line."

# Plain text paragraphs, no markup, at most 2000 characters.
description: |
  TODO: a few short paragraphs of plain text: what the software does, what this
  definition sets up and what is left to the operator.

# One of database, web, tools, devops, storage, monitoring, communication, network, security, other.
# TODO: choose the category
category: other

# The software's https homepage.
homepage: https://memcached.org/

# The software's license, as its SPDX name (BSD-2-Clause, MIT, Apache-2.0, ...).
license: TODO

# A .svg or .png next to this file, at most 64 KB; leave it out for none.
icon: ""

# FreeBSD only, 15.1 or later.
image:
  os: freebsd
  release_min: "15.1"

# Installed from the host with pkg -r into the jail root: repo ports (the FreeBSD
# repository, the default) or jailops (pkg.jailops.net). version is what you tested
# against, not a pin. The installer runs ldconfig in the jail afterwards itself.
packages:
  - name: memcached
    repo: ports
    version: "1.6.39"

# rc.d scripts, in start order. The installer writes <name>_enable="YES" itself; rc holds
# the other rc.conf knobs. start: false enables a service without starting it. A start
# runs through a wrapper that detaches the output, so an rc script that leaves a
# daemon(8) supervisor behind does not hang the installation.
services:
  - name: memcached
    rc:
      memcached_flags: "-l 0.0.0.0 -m 64"
    start: true

# What the services listen on, declared honestly (proto tcp or udp; say so when a port
# listens on localhost only). expose: true asks for forwarding, which is not done yet.
ports:
  - name: memcached
    port: 11211
    proto: tcp
    description: Listened on by memcached.
    expose: false

# Every directory that holds state. Each becomes a volume of its own (snapshotted, backed
# up, kept across upgrades), created empty and owned by root: chown it in setup.
# TODO: every directory that holds state needs a data path: only the jail's volumes were found
data:
  - path: /var/db/memcached

# Files a person edits, and the service to restart after a change.
# TODO: keep the files a person edits, with the service each one restarts
config: []

# Log files or directories, below /var/log, /var/db or /usr/local.
# TODO: no log file of the app was found; name where it logs (many log to /var/log/messages), or [] for none
logs: []

# Run once in the jail after the packages and rc.conf, before the services: argv with
# an absolute program path, no shell, no network. pkg -r installs from the host, where
# the package's user does not exist, so the directories a package gives to its user
# come out owned by root: mkdir -p and chown them here, the data volumes too.
# TODO: check the owners and add what else must happen once before the first start
setup:
  - [/bin/mkdir, -p, /var/run/memcached]
  - [/usr/sbin/chown, "nobody:nobody", /var/db/memcached, /var/run/memcached]

# true only for a server that needs System V shared memory, semaphores or message
# queues (PostgreSQL's initdb does); the jail then gets namespaces of its own.
sysvipc: false

# How the installer checks that it runs: exactly one of tcp: PORT, http: {port, path,
# status} or exec: [argv in the jail], retried for wait_seconds (at most 300). null only
# for an app that starts none of its services; say why in the description.
# TODO: check that this test fits: a tcp connection says only that something listens; http or exec can say more
health:
  tcp: 11211
  wait_seconds: 60

# Hints for the jail's limits (memory and disk sizes, cpu a count), not limits.
resources:
  memory: 256M
  disk: 2G
  cpu: "1"

# Shown after the installation: the first-run steps and how to connect.
notes: |
  TODO: what to do after the installation: the first-run steps, how to connect.

$ jailops app export cache -o <dir>/cache.yaml
Wrote <dir>/cache.yaml, drafted from jail cache, with 10 line(s) marked TODO.
Next: fill in the marked lines and run 'jailops app validate <dir>/cache.yaml'.

$ jailops app validate <dir>/cache.yaml
